'ECON-MG-EASY-10':{q:'Two neighbors specialize in the chores for which each gives up less valuable alternative work, then exchange help. Why can both benefit?',options:['Specialization can raise combined output available to share','Exchange makes both neighbors completely self-sufficient','Scarcity disappears once the chores are divided','The faster neighbor must perform every chore'],answer:'Specialization can raise combined output available to share',type:'application',skill:'conceptual_gains_from_trade',depth:'CONCEPTUAL_GAINS',commonError:'Treating trade as zero-sum or assigning every task to the fastest producer.',feedback:'Different opportunity costs can make specialization and voluntary exchange beneficial.'},
'P52B-TRADE-E-001':{q:'Mira gives up 1 report to prepare a presentation; Sol gives up 3 reports. Which fact determines comparative advantage in presentations?',options:['Mira gives up fewer reports per presentation','Sol may complete more total tasks in a day','Mira charges a lower money price after exchange','Sol has a higher wage in an unrelated job'],answer:'Mira gives up fewer reports per presentation',type:'application',skill:'comparative_advantage_definition',depth:'ABSOLUTE_VS_COMPARATIVE',commonError:'Using total output, wages, or prices instead of opportunity cost.',feedback:'Comparative advantage belongs to the lower-opportunity-cost producer.'},
'P52B-TRADE-E-002':{q:'With the same resources, East Farm can harvest 12 crates while West Farm can harvest 8. What does this establish?',options:['East Farm has an absolute advantage in harvesting crates','East Farm must have comparative advantage in every crop','West Farm has the lower opportunity cost of harvesting','Both farms gain equally from every possible exchange'],answer:'East Farm has an absolute advantage in harvesting crates',type:'application',skill:'absolute_advantage_definition',depth:'ABSOLUTE_VS_COMPARATIVE',commonError:'Inferring comparative advantage from productivity alone.',feedback:'Greater output from the same resources establishes absolute, not necessarily comparative, advantage.'},
'P52B-TRADE-H-005':{q:'A region can obtain one turbine by giving up 3 tons of grain through exchange or 5 tons by producing it internally. Which reasoning supports exchange?',options:['Importing the turbine has the lower opportunity cost in grain','Producing internally must be better because it avoids trade','The money price eliminates the need to compare resources','Exchange guarantees equal gains for every resident'],answer:'Importing the turbine has the lower opportunity cost in grain',type:'application',skill:'opportunity_cost_trade',depth:'TERMS_OF_TRADE',commonError:'Preferring self-sufficiency without comparing opportunity costs.',feedback:'Exchange uses resources worth 3 tons rather than 5, freeing 2 tons for other uses.'},
'P52B-TRADE-H-006':{q:'Two firms specialize and create 40 extra units of combined output. A proposed exchange gives all 40 extra units to one firm. What is the strongest conclusion?',options:['Specialization created aggregate gains, but the proposed distribution may not benefit both firms','Comparative advantage is false whenever gains are unequal','Any increase in combined output makes every exchange mutually beneficial','Equal gains are required before specialization can raise output'],answer:'Specialization created aggregate gains, but the proposed distribution may not benefit both firms',type:'application',skill:'distribution_of_trade_gains',depth:'DISTRIBUTION_OF_GAINS',commonError:'Confusing total gains with mutual benefit under a particular exchange.',feedback:'Higher combined output creates potential gains; terms of trade determine how those gains are divided.'},
'P52B-TRADE-EL-003':{q:'A trade rate equals the exporter’s opportunity cost exactly but remains below the importer’s opportunity cost. Which evaluation is precise?',options:['The exporter is indifferent while the importer captures the modeled gain','The exporter captures every gain because it supplies the good','Both parties receive strict and exactly equal gains','Neither party can gain because the rate is on one boundary'],answer:'The exporter is indifferent while the importer captures the modeled gain',type:'application',skill:'terms_of_trade_distribution',depth:'DISTRIBUTION_OF_GAINS',commonError:'Calling a boundary rate a strict mutual gain or assuming gains divide equally.',feedback:'At its own opportunity cost the exporter is indifferent; the importer still saves resources.'}
,'P52B-TRADE-BR-001':{q:'A project lead is faster at every task but delegates data entry. Which concept explains the choice?',options:['A price ceiling that limits payment for outsourced data entry','Absolute advantage because the lead is faster at data entry','Comparative advantage based on the opportunity cost of the lead\'s time','Elimination of scarce time after the work is delegated'],answer:'Comparative advantage based on the opportunity cost of the lead\'s time',type:'bridge',skill:'comparative_advantage_application',depth:'ABSOLUTE_VS_COMPARATIVE',commonError:'Using absolute productivity instead of the opportunity cost of the lead\'s time.',feedback:'Delegation can be efficient when the lead has a higher opportunity cost of doing data entry.'},
'P52B-TRADE-BR-002':{q:'A city can buy snowplow service from a neighboring county for less than the resources required to provide it internally. What does opportunity-cost reasoning suggest?',options:['The city should remain self-sufficient even at a higher resource cost','The neighboring county cannot benefit from any voluntary contract','Money prices make the city\'s forgone alternatives irrelevant','Contracting can free city resources for other services'],answer:'Contracting can free city resources for other services',type:'bridge',skill:'opportunity_cost_trade',depth:'SIMPLE_OPPORTUNITY_COST',commonError:'Ignoring the internal resources forgone when the city provides the service itself.',feedback:'Buying the service at lower opportunity cost frees scarce city resources for other uses.'}
